  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWidespread overcast skies blanket the region early this morning per \nsatl imagery as a long fetch of low to mid level moisture continue \nto stream northward from the Central Plains into the Northern \nPlains. As a result, temperatures have remained relatively steady in \nthe mid 30s to low 40s. Early morning precip returns off the \nregional radar mosaic have dispersed to the north and east across \nour forecast area. This has led to a break in the light rain but it \nwill only be a temporary break as more rain is expected to break out \ntoday and tonight.\n\nThe upper flow pattern across the region remains driven by an upper \nlow across the Central Rockies. This system will deliver the next \nbout of moisture into our forecast area today into tonight. Models \nremain in fair agreement in tracking the mid/upper low features of \nthis system into the NE Panhandle during the day and into central SD \nthis evening into the overnight. Diffluent flow aloft and mid level \nforcing will spread precip northward becoming more widespread across \ncentral and eastern SD as the day turn into night. The steady rains \nand low clouds will bring local visibilities down to around 2 miles \nor less this afternoon into this evening. \n\nTemps at the sfc and aloft remain a critical factor in determining \nthe p-type our forecast area sees. BUFKIT soundings across portions \nof central and northeast SD and west central MN maintain rain for \nmost of this storm system. However, north central SD will see a bit \nmore dynamic cooling that should be enough to lead to frozen precip \nor snowfall mixing in with the rain at some point this afternoon \ninto this evening. The late day hours into tonight will be a more \nfavorable period for that mix to turn to all snow. EC ENS p-type \nmeteograms at various locations across our CWA agree with this \nscenario. The majority of ensemble members are more confident in \nsnowfall basically north and west of a Pierre to Aberdeen line this \nafternoon into tonight versus anywhere east and south of that line. \nAs far as accumulation potential, confidence remains low. The 25th \nto 75th percentiles continue to show nothing up to 2 inches. The \n90th percentile amounts are as high as 3 to 5 inches. Given the time \nof year, a recent stretch above normal temperatures and near sfc \nsoil temps in the 40s, any noteworthy accumulations may be hard to \ncome by. Still, can't rule out some light or minor accumulations on \ngrassy areas especially once we get to the end of the day into this \nevening after sunset.\n\nThis system will eventually shift out of our region by early Tuesday \nmorning with drier conditions returning. However, it will be quickly \nfollowed up by another upper low tracking northeast out of the \nNorthern High Plains into parts of ND and southern Canada on \nWednesday. The sfc low and associated fronts tracking east across \nthe region could touch off a few showers in eastern SD and western \nMN as the cold front passes through on Wednesday, but most of the \nmoisture associated with this system should remain to our north and \neast. Temperatures will start to modify midweek as quasi-zonal upper \nflow sets up. Upper ridging building into the Northern Plains toward \nthe end of the week will lead to a return of above normal temps.",
